  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Tuesday)\nIssued at 107 PM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery highlights a closed low centered in southern \nNV. Further upstream, a strong vort max is located just off the BC\ncoastline with upper level moisture streaming into western \nCanada. Surface high pressure has settled across eastern \nMT/western ND with temperatures across the forecast area in the \n40s. Low clouds will linger through the afternoon, limiting high \ntemperature potential to the mid to upper 40s for most of the area\nand low to mid 50s across southern SD. Temperatures are forecast \nto fall below freezing across portions of Harding, Perkins, and \nZiebach counties, particularly closer to the SD/ND border; have \nissued a Freeze Warning for these counties. Cloud cover may be a \nlimiting factor, however, enough clearing is expected across this \narea for temperatures to bottom out. South of the warned area, \ncloud cover is expected to keep temperatures above freezing and \nlimit the potential of frost development.\n\nChances for precipitation increase Wednesday morning into Thursday\nas the closed low ejects into the Central Plains. Most of the rain\nwill be confined to areas along and south of I-90 where 0.5-0.75\"+\nof rain possible for southern Campbell/Weston counties in WY and\nareas along the SD/NE border.\n\nThe northern stream wave that is currently located off the BC coast \nwill swing through southern Canada into the Northern Plains and \nUpper Midwest late Wednesday into Thursday with a cold front to push \nthrough Thursday. As has been the case for a few days, considerable\nspread remains among deterministic and ensemble guidance regarding\nthe ultimate strength of the winds behind the front. The strongest\ncore of 850-750 mb winds are forecast to remain displaced to our \nnorth and east, coinciding with the greatest cold air advection. \nForecast soundings highlight potential for Wind Advisory level \nwinds (wind gusts > 45 mph) across northwestern SD through the \nwestern SD plains Thursday afternoon. Overall forecast confidence\nremains low, however. The NBM continues to forecast High Wind\nstrength winds(gusts > 60 mph), which seem overdone and unlikely \ngiven the ingredients that will be in place.\n\nClearing skies and cold air behind the front will result in \nwidespread sub-freezing to freezing temperatures across the forecast \narea Thursday night into Friday morning. Much warmer this weekend \nwith highs in the 60s and 70s as upper ridging builds over the \nwestern half of the US.",
